# Clean Code: Boundaries

Change happens at boundaries. Very few places in the codebase should refer directly to third-party code.

- **Don't pass boundary interfaces (e.g., `Map`) around your system.** Encapsulate them; expose only the narrow API your app needs. Keep casting/generics as hidden implementation details.

```java
// GOOD — encapsulate the boundary
public class Sensors {
  private Map sensors = new HashMap();
  public Sensor getById(String id) { return (Sensor) sensors.get(id); }
}
```

- **Write learning tests** to explore a new library in isolation — they cost nothing (you must learn it anyway), document expected usage, and detect breaking changes on upgrades.
- **For code that doesn't exist yet, define the interface you wish you had.** Keep moving; bridge to the real API later with an **Adapter** (the single place to change). This also gives a clean **seam** for fakes in tests.
- **Depend on what you control, not what you don't.** Wrap or adapt at every boundary so internal code speaks one consistent vocabulary.

```java
// Define your own interface before the vendor API exists
public interface Transmitter {
  void transmit(Frequency frequency, DataStream stream);
}
// Later: TransmitterAdapter implements Transmitter over the real API
```
